#answer("ch6-yt1")[Variables $delta_k in {0,1}$ (line $k$ runs) and $q_k >= 0$ (hectolitres on line $k$). Objective $max sum_k (p_k q_k - f_k delta_k)$. Limits $L_k delta_k <= q_k <= U_k delta_k$. Demand $sum_k q_k <= D$. Line 3 needs line 1: $delta_3 <= delta_1$.]

#answer("ch6-a")[(a) $4 delta_j <= h_j <= 16 delta_j$. (b) $x <= U delta$ makes $delta = 0$ mean "not used"; $x >= L delta$ makes $delta = 1$ mean "used at least $L$". (c) If $U$ is smaller than a value $x$ can legitimately take, $delta = 1$ wrongly forbids it. A huge $U$ is correct but weakens the LP relaxation (fractional $delta$ like $x slash U$ becomes almost free), which slows branch & bound.]

#answer("ch6-b")[(a) $3 x_1 + 2 x_2 >= 12 - M(1 - delta)$. With $delta = 0$ we need $3 x_1 + 2 x_2 >= 12 - M$ for all $x$; the smallest left side is 0, so $M = 12$: $3 x_1 + 2 x_2 >= 12 delta$. (b) If the pair is not both in class $j$, the right side is $C_(i k) + 1$ or more, which is $>= 1 >= z$ because $C_(i k) >= 0$ and $z <= 1$. (c) Some legitimate solutions become infeasible when $delta = 0$ (or $1$): the optimum can be wrong.]

#answer("ch6-w1")[(a) $x <= 50 delta$; (b) $x >= 5 delta$; (c) $10 delta <= x <= 50 delta$.]

#answer("ch6-w2")[$x + y <= 6 + M delta$; with $delta = 1$ it must hold for all $x, y$, and $max(x + y) = 20$, so $M = 14$: $x + y <= 6 + 14 delta$.]

#answer("ilp-sheet-1")[Start from the LP of Worked example 1.1 (variables $x_1, dots, x_5$, $y$). *New variables:* $delta_i in {0,1}$, oil $i$ is used. *Constraints:* $x_i <= 200 delta_i$ ($i = 1, 2$) and $x_i <= 250 delta_i$ ($i = 3, 4, 5$) — the refining capacities are the natural bounds; $x_i >= 20 delta_i$; $sum_i delta_i >= 3$; $delta_1 <= delta_5$ and $delta_2 <= delta_5$ ("VEG 1 or VEG 2 ⇒ NON 3" split into two implications). CPLEX: profit 17 433.33 € (down from 17 592.59 € in the LP), with 153.33 t of VEG 1, 46.67 t of VEG 2, 230 t of NON 2 and the forced 20 t of NON 3.]

#answer("ilp-sheet-8")[(a) *Variables:* tons on every possible arc: $a_(f d)$ factory→depot, $b_(f k)$ factory→customer, $g_(d k)$ depot→customer (only where a cost is given). *Objective:* minimize the sum of cost × tons. *Constraints:* factory capacity, $sum_d a_(f d) + sum_k b_(f k) <= "cap"_f$; depot balance, $sum_f a_(f d) = sum_k g_(d k)$; depot throughput, $sum_k g_(d k) <= "thr"_d$; customer demand, $sum_f b_(f k) + sum_d g_(d k) = "req"_k$. Optimum 198.5 k£ per month. (b) *New binaries:* $o_d$ = depot $d$ open, for Newcastle, Exeter, Bristol, Northampton; $e$ = Birmingham enlarged. Throughput becomes $sum_k g_(d k) <= "thr"_d o_d$ for those four and $<= 50 + 20 e$ (thousand t) for Birmingham. At most four depots: $o_"New" + o_"Exe" + o_"Brs" + o_"Nor" + 2 <= 4$ (Birmingham and London stay). Objective adds $12 o_"Brs" + 4 o_"Nor" + 3 e - 10(1 - o_"New") - 5(1 - o_"Exe")$ (k£). Optimum 174 k£: close Newcastle, open Northampton, enlarge Birmingham, keep Exeter, no Bristol. It cannot be an LP: with $o_d in [0, 1]$ the model would "half-open" a depot and pay half its cost, which is not a real option.]

#answer("ilp-sheet-11")[*Variables:* $delta_(i j) in {0,1}$, department $i$ in city $j$; $gamma_(i j k l) in {0,1}$ for $i < k$ with $C_(i k) > 0$, "$i$ in $j$ and $k$ in $l$". *Constraints:* $sum_j delta_(i j) = 1$ for each $i$; $sum_i delta_(i j) <= 3$ for each $j$; $gamma_(i j k l) >= delta_(i j) + delta_(k l) - 1$ (the product; $gamma <= delta$ is not needed because $gamma$ only adds cost). *Objective:* $min sum C_(i k) D_(j l) gamma_(i j k l) - sum_(i, j) B_(i j) delta_(i j)$ (k€; $C$ in thousands × € = k€). Optimum $-14.9$ k€, i.e. a net yearly gain of 14.9 k€: A and D to Bristol, B, C and E to Brighton (with $D_"Brighton,Brighton" = 5$; checked by enumerating all $3^5$ placements).]

#answer("ilp-sheet-12")[*Variables:* $n_(k t) in {0, dots, N_k}$ generators of type $k$ working in period $t$; $x_(k t) >= 0$ total output of type $k$ (MW); $s_(k t) >= 0$ integer, generators of type $k$ started at the start of $t$. *Constraints:* demand $sum_k x_(k t) >= D_t$; output limits $m_k n_(k t) <= x_(k t) <= M_k n_(k t)$; reserve $sum_k M_k n_(k t) >= 1.15 D_t$; start-ups $s_(k t) >= n_(k t) - n_(k, t-1)$ with period 0 = period 5 (the day repeats). *Objective:* $min sum_(k, t) h_t [E_k n_(k t) + F_k (x_(k t) - m_k n_(k t))] + sum_(k, t) G_k s_(k t)$, with $h_t$ = 6, 3, 6, 3, 6 hours, $E_k$ the hourly cost at minimum, $F_k$ the hourly cost per MW above minimum and $G_k$ the start-up cost. Optimum 988 540 € per day (H. P. Williams' "tariff rates" value); all 12 type-1 units always run; type 2 runs 3, 8, 8, 9, 9 units; two type-3 units run only from 3 to 6 p.m. If instead all units are off before midnight (no repetition), the optimum is 1 015 150 €.]

#answer("jan25-q2bd")[(b) For each class $j$: $sum_(i in G) x_(i j) >= 2 sum_(i in B) x_(i j)$. (c) The objective $max min_j min{C_(i k) : i < k, x_(i j) = x_(k j) = 1}$ is not linear. Introduce a real variable $z$, "minimum compatibility within classes", maximize $z$, and add $z <= 1$ and, for all $j$ and all $i < k$, $z <= C_(i k) + 2 - x_(i j) - x_(k j)$, i.e. $x_(i j) and x_(k j) -> z <= C_(i k)$. (d) Yes: each student in exactly one class, $sum_(j=1)^m x_(i j) = 1$ for every $i$. Checked against brute force on 25 random instances (6 students, 2 classes). Note that $z <= 1$ also covers the corner case of classes with fewer than two students (no pair constrains $z$).]

